                <pb facs="0797" n="699" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg699"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Everett to Mr.
                            Hay.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States,<lb/>Guatemala and
                                Honduras</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-11-09"><hi rendition="#italic">November 9,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 459.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that on the
                    6th instant I received a telegram, a copy of which I inclose herewith, from the
                    minister of foreign affairs of Honduras informing me that his Government had
                    decided to pay the indemnity demanded in the Pears case, and that drafts for the
                    amount would be sent me in due course.</p>
                <p>A copy of my note of October 11, to which he refers, was inclosed in my dispatch
                    to the Department, No. 446, of October 12.</p>
                <p>I at once acknowledged the receipt of above telegram in a telegram dated the 6th
                    instant, a copy of which I inclose herewith, and communicated the facts to the
                    Department the same day in a cablegram.</p>
                <p>I am now awaiting the arrival of the promised drafts, which will be forwarded to
                    the Department as soon as received.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sidney B. Everett</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Chargé d’Affaires ad interim.</hi></signed></closer>



